 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels in the chunk, as each voxel is processed once and each face checks a constant number of neighbors (8 for smooth lighting).
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(V + I)$ where $V$ is the number of vertices and $I$ is the number of indices. The vertex buffer stores 5 attributes per vertex.
-*   **Performance:** The method is designed for Burst compilation in Unity, allowing parallel processing of chunks [S1, unofficial]. Smooth lighting increases the computational cost per face due to 8 neighbor samples and averaging, but provides smoother visual transitions [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Handles both opaque and transparent voxels; supports smooth lighting for better aesthetics; efficient neighbor sampling for boundary chunks.
-*   **Limitations:** Boundary chunks require careful handling of neighbor data to avoid artifacts; smooth lighting may be computationally expensive for very large chunks if not parallelized.
-*   **When to Use:** When visual quality (smooth lighting) is prioritized over raw vertex count optimization.
-*   **When Not to Use:** When memory bandwidth is the primary bottleneck and flat lighting suffices, or when using optimized indexed geometry techniques that reduce vertex count further.
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(V \cdot F \cdot L)$, where $V$ is the number of voxels in the chunk, $F=6$ is the number of faces, and $L$ is the cost of lighting calculation (constant for flat, higher for smooth due to 8 neighbor samples).
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(V)$ for vertex and index storage, proportional to the surface area of the voxel chunk.
+*   **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Scaling:** Scales linearly with chunk size. Chunk dimensions are fixed at $16 \times 256 \times 16$ [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Smooth lighting reduces the "blocky" appearance of voxel worlds; separate alpha/opaque buffers allow for correct transparency rendering.
+*   **Limitations:** Boundary chunks require complex neighbor data resolution; smooth lighting increases CPU/GPU job cost due to multiple neighbor samples.
+*   **When to use:** When visual quality of lighting transitions is prioritized over raw meshing speed.
+*   **When not:** When memory bandwidth is constrained or chunks are very large with high surface area.
 
 ## Background
